WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.272 --> 00:00:02.689
(soft music)

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:06.300
- Hello, I'm Jonathan Van Ness,

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:07.947
You may know me from
"Queer Eye" on Netflix,

00:00:07.947 --> 00:00:09.334
"Getting Curious" on Netflix,

00:00:09.334 --> 00:00:11.660
I don't know where you know
me from, but I do know this,

00:00:11.660 --> 00:00:14.660
my new book "Love That Story" is out now,

00:00:14.660 --> 00:00:17.903
and we're gonna do a
little bit of Q and ASMR.

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:26.800
For our first selection,
it's so hard to whisper,

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:28.043
I'm gonna do it now though.

00:00:28.043 --> 00:00:30.597
"Filming "Queer Eye"", oh my
gosh, I can't do it anymore,

00:00:30.597 --> 00:00:32.571
"is an incredible opportunity

00:00:32.571 --> 00:00:35.342
to delve into someone else's life,

00:00:35.342 --> 00:00:39.280
and in every episode, I give
myself over to each hero

00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:41.695
and help in any way I can.

00:00:41.695 --> 00:00:46.480
I love my job, and I also
love that the show allows me

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:48.297
to be a hairdresser, a comedian,

00:00:48.297 --> 00:00:50.740
and an overall entertainer.

00:00:50.740 --> 00:00:53.230
But as I've spent more time
in these different careers,

00:00:53.230 --> 00:00:56.510
I've realized that I can't
stop and take a moment

00:00:56.510 --> 00:00:58.010
for my personal life,

00:00:58.010 --> 00:01:00.960
even if I'm going through
something incredibly hard.

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:02.630
When I signed on to "Queer Eye",

00:01:02.630 --> 00:01:05.610
I became a beacon of positivity and love.

00:01:05.610 --> 00:01:06.780
And there are a lot of times

00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:08.430
when I have to maintain that strength

00:01:08.430 --> 00:01:11.510
even when I should probably
be taking a little time

00:01:11.510 --> 00:01:14.010
to focus that positivity inward,

00:01:14.010 --> 00:01:16.570
to let myself mourn
when a tragedy happens,

00:01:16.570 --> 00:01:18.477
like what happened to Bug the Second."

00:01:21.779 --> 00:01:23.660
I think what I've learned about grief

00:01:23.660 --> 00:01:25.390
from exploring grief in this chapter

00:01:25.390 --> 00:01:26.700
and then just like the various times

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:28.210
that I've dealt with grief in my life,

00:01:28.210 --> 00:01:30.116
is that there's no one way

00:01:30.116 --> 00:01:32.590
that we're all going to handle grief.

00:01:32.590 --> 00:01:35.180
And it's so individual
for whatever the thing

00:01:35.180 --> 00:01:37.650
that we're grieving is, for
wherever we are in our lives,

00:01:37.650 --> 00:01:38.790
and I think it's important for us

00:01:38.790 --> 00:01:40.190
to like release the judgment

00:01:40.190 --> 00:01:42.910
and release the shame around how we grieve

00:01:42.910 --> 00:01:45.260
'cause sometimes you just
have to impulse adopt

00:01:45.260 --> 00:01:48.296
like two kittens and that's okay.

00:01:48.296 --> 00:01:50.472
For our second section,

00:01:50.472 --> 00:01:55.472
"My imposter syndrome would sneak up."

00:01:55.690 --> 00:01:57.377
Oh my gosh, I made it five words.

00:01:57.377 --> 00:02:01.090
"On me most often while
researching different cities

00:02:01.090 --> 00:02:03.490
I was scheduled to perform in.

00:02:03.490 --> 00:02:06.160
I planned to talk about
the venues. I'd be at,

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:08.370
find out what the comedy
scene was like there

00:02:08.370 --> 00:02:11.840
and incorporate a little
local flare into my set.

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:15.600
But then I think about
how many other comedians

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:19.230
had spent their whole lives
trying to do the same thing,

00:02:19.230 --> 00:02:20.740
but never got the chance.

00:02:20.740 --> 00:02:22.630
At smaller venues, where I was booked

00:02:22.630 --> 00:02:25.240
alongside more established comedians,

00:02:25.240 --> 00:02:26.660
I'd sometimes get paranoid

00:02:26.660 --> 00:02:28.430
that they were giving serious attitude,

00:02:28.430 --> 00:02:30.212
like who is this blank?

00:02:30.212 --> 00:02:31.110
- [Producer] You can say it.

00:02:31.110 --> 00:02:34.980
- Oh, who is this (beep)
unscripted nobody from "Queer Eye?

00:02:34.980 --> 00:02:36.920
He doesn't deserve to be here."

00:02:36.920 --> 00:02:39.260
Much in the way that
I look at Oksana Baiul

00:02:39.260 --> 00:02:40.270
for her gold medal,

00:02:40.270 --> 00:02:43.690
Nancy was robbed and I
don't care who knows it."

00:02:43.690 --> 00:02:46.310
Thank you for coming to our Q and ASMR,

00:02:46.310 --> 00:02:47.500
with me, Jonathan Van Ness,

00:02:47.500 --> 00:02:49.240
my book "Love That Story" is available now

00:02:49.240 --> 00:02:51.740
at jonathanvanness.com/book.

00:02:51.740 --> 00:02:53.603
Yay, or wherever you get your books, ah.

